#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068352 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10008" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 2x + 3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{1}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{1}}x + {\\color{blue}{1}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{1}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x + {\\color{blue}{1}})^2 + 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-10009" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 12x + 40$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue}{6}}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{40}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{6}}x + {\\color{blue}{6}}^2} - \\, {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{6}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{40}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x - {\\color{blue}{6}})^2 +4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9927" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 + 14x + 49) - 8$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 14x + 49 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 14x + 7^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 7x + 7^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 7)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + 14x + 49) - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 7)^{2} - 8 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-9997" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $(x^2 - 12x + 36) -5$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 12x + 36 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 12x + 6^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2 \\cdot 6x + 6^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 6)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 12x + 36) - 5 &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 6)^{2} - 5 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b36c987-b0af-507f-a72c-cd948bd5d8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-9{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242481} {:answer/id #uuid "3105398b-d43d-5839-ac22-e423d28ca441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+9{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242482} {:answer/id #uuid "c40acf24-bbed-578a-82b7-c430d9955446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242483} {:answer/id #uuid "18fbd864-ed61-5323-a1ee-2d47cca55129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242484} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcd4651-c78c-582c-b6ea-03093f30e481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c40acf24-bbed-578a-82b7-c430d9955446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242483} :answer-field/id #uuid "4690505a-1e03-564c-a186-218e633f3a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242480}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb292616-b4ad-5742-8de0-3d07446bbce4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-140277" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2})+6$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2ay+{a}^{2}=(y-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (y-3{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}-6y+{3}^{2})+6 & = \\\\ (y-3{)}^{2}+6 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c7d182a-28e9-5e31-b367-4092ca7f65c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242488} {:answer/id #uuid "2daf86ec-eb7b-53c7-b040-7403f996dd85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242489} {:answer/id #uuid "725e5336-ba8a-5b26-ab75-eec63b261865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242490} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ccbbd-de95-5e91-86ba-cb0d51ee9cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242491} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b3669-120d-5475-b1c7-762479718f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2daf86ec-eb7b-53c7-b040-7403f996dd85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242489} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7ab9f6-bef8-515d-af14-92cde8db4c6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radi