$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756bfdc4-248e-5c9c-a863-454a359fd995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242537} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1d796b-9602-53b7-b078-a1c9b8d49c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "756bfdc4-248e-5c9c-a863-454a359fd995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242537} :answer-field/id #uuid "192c0903-304b-52d7-87fa-5ea29e26c2e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242536} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a13f07d-d501-5faa-b8ec-d4aacc8ed7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a13f07d-d501-5faa-b8ec-d4aacc8ed7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242540} :answer-field/id #uuid "de4cd0dd-9bca-5cd3-901a-c90bd7322e14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "269a1802-85a1-5cce-b276-875ddfeb0cde" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-250392" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+14x+49)+1=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\