50396" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2})+7=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+10{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2})+7 & = \\\\ (y+10{)}^{2}+7 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4752658-4c94-541f-ba23-ddde0042ff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4752658-4c94-541f-ba23-ddde0042ff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242553} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95ad6e0-28b7-5faf-ae57-73c4c34c2aa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242552} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f838d87a-1de0-539f-9d8b-022c1462c951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f838d87a-1de0-539f-9d8b-022c1462c951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242555} :answer-field/id #uuid "18187f20-ea32-5dcb-bccf-e561ec285b16" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "437c9631-b7a7-5f8d-8cb0-38f1025bd789" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-250397" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3 & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf731f8-8155-534f-bf15-61499aad5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bf731f8-8155-534f-bf15-61499aad5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242558} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b8544a-6a7f-56c7-80d7-7f1316839da8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff623b20-e5af-5fe4-9b1d-13ac30ca2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff623b20-e5af-5fe4-9b1d-13ac30ca2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242560} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1d4ad1-11da-5553-bc47-861011cd00ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45fd3e5c-15dc-54b6-a19b-5c5121a9e8b7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-250428" :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18x+81 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 9x+{9}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2 & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2}+2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117460a2-6c67-539b-afd5-f42bc144e7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242563} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e9a9b6-8e3d-52b8-8f08-c22a5ecdac0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "117460a2-6c67-539b-afd5-f42bc144e7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242563} :answer-field/id #uuid "e686957e-d7f9-5e4c-a147-bccd462f15b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c42a833-ca36-559c-8527-38c840eadc7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c42a833-ca36-559c-8527-38c840eadc7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242566} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd06d6c-7942-556f-863d-09e7b07f918a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a737fd8e-cb6d-589b-bb04-a75a383135e5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-250452" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+22y+131.$\n\n${y}^{2}+22y+131=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+131\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${11}^{2},$ writing the constant term $131$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+{11}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+11{)}^{2}-{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+11{)}^{2}+10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0273277f-c26f-5856-9e22-6afc6e471f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0273277f-c26f-5856-9e22-6afc6e471f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242569} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e1c7e3-fca8-589e-9222-fded93cf61fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed165bd7-7d94-5066-8699-206f7f41ffe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed165bd7-7d94-5066-8699-206f7f41ffe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242571} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e8a61d-0665-56f6-84ad-3ba919a1dad4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "543f6b63-b23f-5799-bcd2-45789ff9ae82" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-250454" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+8y+13.$\n\n${y}^{2}+8y+13=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+13\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $13$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+4{)}^{2}-{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+4{)}^{2}-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f6b8ab-15a8-53bc-86b2-36ebf09a5c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242574} {:answer/id #uuid "6abd2a16-a75f-5801-a675-30fc82fce96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abd2a16-a75f-5801-a675-30fc82fce96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242575} :answer-field/id #uuid "114a5f2e-3c1a-