r/value "p=2,\\,q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242585} {:answer/id #uuid "cd018992-4555-5a70-90be-3c245783cc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=4,\\,q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242586} {:answer/id #uuid "551ba5e9-93e5-5533-9d96-a157c083f671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=4,\\,q=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242587} {:answer/id #uuid "20fe7a67-99a1-5a81-a64e-2f1e176a90fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2,\\,q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242588} {:answer/id #uuid "b25dc639-0527-5e75-b2eb-43b0e72952a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2,\\,q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daaab2c4-59ff-537d-b0c5-c4b9e40f278f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2,\\,q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242585} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0fe990-54a1-5b0a-8d68-fb7b0c54c7a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a00bfa2-00e3-5d34-bf9b-acf1bd55e3fe" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-25238" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+4x+5$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70e5348c-b23e-5dbd-ad42-1e88a28c8080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242592} {:answer/id #uuid "c961ba00-4415-51c2-a9fa-d5d503c8919a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\text{ }q=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242593} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d22d74-b8e4-504c-8234-892dcb916479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242594} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9ec5c2-55c3-5cdc-9442-9ad1f20354c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242595} {:answer/id #uuid "a334d4f7-c953-51b7-8e6b-3e2b4128dce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-2,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d22d74-b8e4-504c-8234-892dcb916479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\text{ }q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242594} :answer-field/id #uuid "9612376e-5a35-5157-bd37-f2203c31650c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa0b78e6-1d13-58b6-af73-5b3d97ee3c04" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-3662" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}-2x$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baf2e671-f47e-5a46-83f7-4aa7f1d8b981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242599} {:answer/id #uuid "31c80d33-ca32-5931-8f89-b4b935a36b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242600} {:answer/id #uuid "31354583-b548-5d4b-af6c-b4587082da26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} {:answer/id #uuid "85870e7b-0e89-5e86-9039-2f61a04c1867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242602} {:answer/id #uuid "a743bf18-39dc-5ab0-b2bf-355b4eff24b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31354583-b548-5d4b-af6c-b4587082da26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} :answer-field/id #uuid "863b5193-9c26-58a2-994d-3b3b68dad23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c955036-6499-5619-8eaf-c5e910d251ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-3667" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+12x+35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6{)}^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6{)}^{2}-1.\n$$"}]}
